      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The NK(2) receptor (member of the tachykinin receptor family) is mainly located in the smooth muscle of the urinary, respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts, with limited presence in the CNS. This has raised interest in tachykinin NK(2) receptor antagonists for the treatment of urological disorders, asthma. This review outlines progress done after 1998 in the field of NK(2) small molecule antagonists, both acting on the NK(1)/NK(2), NK(2)/NK(3), NK(1)/NK(2)/NK(3) receptors and selective for the NK(2) one."},
